WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to the 20th anniversary

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.690
celebration,
extravaganza, party,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.720
celebrating 20 years of
Hedwig and the Angry Inch.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.830
My name is Peppermint and I know
if you are anything like me,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.330
then you know exactly
where you were

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.297
the first time you ever heard
of Hedwig and the Angry Inch.

00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:21.630
I was working as a make-up
artist at the make-up counter

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.740
during the day for MAC Cosmetics
when "Angry Inch" the lipstick

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.480
came out to celebrate the film.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.020
And at night, I was
working at Don Hill's

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:37.080
in drag as a superstar--
a future superstar.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.290
And I had no idea that
20 years later I'd

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.760
be here sitting with
the creators, the stars,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.160
and the luminaries
of this very, very

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:57.300
transformative story, piece
of art, this cult classic.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.760
I was just beginning my
gender journey myself,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.040
and I have so many
people to thank,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.269
and they are all pretty
much in this panel.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.770
So I'm going to jump right in.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.000
He is a Tony award winning
American writer, director,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.990
performer, songwriter,
singer, genius.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.060
He directed and
starred and wrote,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.520
with Stephen Trask,
the musical Hedwig

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.630
and the Angry Inch and its
subsequent film adaptation.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.770
Please welcome my friend, John
Cameron Mitchell. (SINGING)

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.390
Welcome, John!

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.220
(SINGING) Thank you!

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:29.790
OK.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.660
And hold up.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.050
We have a Grammy Award-nominated
composer, musician,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.420
and lyricist who
composed all the music

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.230
and lyrics for the
musical and the film.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.570
Please welcome Stephen Trask.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.750
Thank you.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.210
It's nice to be here.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.520
John and Stephen, take us
back to the very beginning.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.340
How did Hedwig even come about?

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.270
The short thing is we met on an
airplane from LA to New York,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.400
I believe.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.730
Weirdly, we both had
connections with New Line Cinema

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.730
who eventually made the film.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.250
Stephen was reading
scripts for New Line.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.740
He had some family
members who worked there.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.419
I had just done a
film called Book

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.300
of Love directed by the
head of New Line, Bob Shaye.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.120
And we just started talking.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.730
When Harry Met Sally was
on the screen, and we both

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.920
were the only two
people not watching it.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.470
He had a Fassbender
biography, which

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.270
was on the seat between us,
which was an ice breaker.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.220
He's not usually
breaking any ice.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.000
He's making ice.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.510
It was three years later when
we kept bumping into each other.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.290
And I had an idea
for a rock musical,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.910
and we saw each other's work.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:44.700
I saw his band Cheater at CBGB's
and he saw me in Hello Again

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.290
at Lincoln Center.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.450
Peanut butter, chocolate, we
got to do something together.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.500
So the Hedwig character
wasn't really there.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.990
I think in the
back of John's mind

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.680
he probably wanted to
base a character on Helga,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:02:59.365
his babysitter.

00:02:59.365 --> 00:03:01.740
But when we started working,
this character wasn't there.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.070
But I was basically
doing a Hedwig style

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.608
show with a rock band and
drag queens at Squeeze Box

00:03:07.608 --> 00:03:08.400
every Friday night.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.790
And I was the music director.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.332
I knew "The Origin
Of Love" was going

00:03:11.332 --> 00:03:13.850
to be the central
metaphor of the story.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:14.850
That was the first song.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:15.815
It comes from Plato.

00:03:15.815 --> 00:03:18.630
It is this story of
us being cut in half

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.700
and seeking our other halves.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.130
I gave Stephen the
text from Plato

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.670
and he came back
with that song whole.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.410
Nothing changed
from that moment.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.080
It was just like the
perfect first song.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.400
That's basically what we had.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.470
And then, so John started
telling me about Helga.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.160
And I said, well, why
don't we make Helga

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.370
into a drag character
but she used

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.940
to be a singer who used to
perform with our rock star who

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.050
was the lead character.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.390
And John was like, I don't--

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.225
you were a little
reluctant to do it,

00:03:50.225 --> 00:03:52.350
and there was a little
resistance to John doing it,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.180
but we just kind of
poured everything

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.410
into that first show,
which was really a riot.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.970
I mean it was just--

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.400
We really took our
time in creating

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.580
a 30 minute mini-musical.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.290
SqueezeBox was a magical place.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.780
And, Peppermint,
we actually met--

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:10.460
At Don Hill's!

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:10.960
Yeah.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.990
--at Don Hill's, which was the
club where SqueezeBox happened.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.540
And it was a
legendary rock club.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.990
And Michael Schmidt, who was
the friend of Arianne Phillips,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:19.652
had this club.

00:04:19.652 --> 00:04:21.360
He just wanted a place
he could hang out.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.010
We're all like,
let's get a place

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.730
where some rock and
roll, drags, mostly

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.280
queer but everyone's welcome.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.200
No bad behavior.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.440
This is a new thing
for the early 90s.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.470
It was Stephen
and my dream club.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.930
It was a dream club for--

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.050
I wouldn't say a lot of people,
because a lot of gay men

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:43.740
went to clubs.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.160
But it was for all the gay
men who didn't like all the--

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.060
Jealousy.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:49.560
Exactly.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.880
And this was the place
that we wanted to be.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.127
And there was 1,000 of us every
Friday night in this club,

00:04:57.127 --> 00:04:57.960
and it just became--

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.200
It was so exciting.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.150
It was like a home.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.150
And we met friends that
are friends forever.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.600
And amazing drag performers
who hadn't used their voices.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.658
They were used to lip syncing.

00:05:08.658 --> 00:05:10.200
Suddenly, they found
that they didn't

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.500
have to sing like Patti
LaBelle when they're

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.270
singing rock and roll and punk.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.220
And they already were punk
and didn't even know it.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.690
So you would get Mistress
Formika, Sherry Vine,

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.840
Lady Bunny who suddenly
she's singing Kate Bush

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.420
and people are screaming
like no one ever did for her.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.070
But we were both learning
from these incredible innate

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:32.980
performances.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.540
Even Pat Briggs, who did this
character called Torment.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.460
And Jimmy James, who is used
to doing Marilyn Monroe.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.160
But now it's Marilyn Monroe
singing "Stairway to Heaven."

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:42.720
Led Zeppelin.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:43.470
Yeah.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.280
It was unbelievable.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.010
And it was a ferment.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.030
And I was learning.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.880
I was learning from the great
Jayne County, Justin Vivian

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:52.380
Bond.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:57.030
These are legends of
drag and trans rock.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.200
That filled us up.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.220
Taught us.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:00.990
And I was just a
little baby drag queen.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.985
I had to learn four or five
different songs every week.

00:06:05.985 --> 00:06:09.060
It could be everything
from the Buzzcocks

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.760
to someone would want to do
a punk version of a Barbra

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:12.930
Streisand song.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.690
Or a Depeche Mode song.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.390
It was just every week it
was a new challenge musically

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:22.380
and it was a lot of growth
for me as a musician.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.010
It's funny.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:27.720
After our first gig, the
scouts for Woodstock '94,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.330
the reboot of
Woodstock was there.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.080
And they were like,
you're amazing!

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.740
We want you to
perform at Woodstock.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.640
And I was like, what?

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:36.307
I couldn't hear.

00:06:36.307 --> 00:06:37.260
I was like Wigstock?

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:37.830
Great.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.000
I love Wigstock.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.650
No, Woodstock?

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.650
And I don't know what happened,
but I think SqueezeBox --

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.690
the other queens were mad at me.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.490
Because I wasn't a
real drag queen and I

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.310
get the invitation to Woodstock.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.780
I wanted a group of
people from SqueezeBox,

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.880
but they couldn't find dressing
rooms or helicopters demanded

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.140
by Mistress Formika.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.700
So that never happened.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.050
But it was the beginning.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.530
It was the birth, and we
kept doing gigs in clubs.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.780
We wanted to keep it
out of the theater

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.240
because the theater tended
to tone everything down,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.790
rock and roll wise.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:15.630
So we developed
eventually for the theater

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.930
over four years at
Westbeth, at the Palladium,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:26.760
at Fire Island, porch parties
where everyone walked away.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.660
We lived Hedwig's life.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:29.790
But then we finally--

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:34.110
with the great help of Peter
Askin our amazing director,

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.970
our first director
and producer--

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.910
we eventually landed in
the Jane Street Theater

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.900
which he built for Hedwig
because every theater in town

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.310
rejected us.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.340
Public Theater, New York Theatre
Workshop, nobody wanted us.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.330
We were too weird.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.200
We were too drag.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.610
We were too punk.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.990
I was a fake drag queen,
a fake rock singer,

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.590
and a fake stand-up
comedian, but it was still

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.190
too much for them.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:59.940
So we had to build a
theater for us to--

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.760
And it was so custom-made that
at the height of the stage

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.337
was set to the height
of John in heels,

00:08:06.337 --> 00:08:07.920
and then people went
and sat out where

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.570
the audience would
sit and said, let's

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.250
make the stage a little higher.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:12.930
It was that custom.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.080
Yeah, we built it
for Hedwig's wig.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.070
By that point, we had brought in
Miriam's Shor who just blew us

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:25.680
away in an unexpected audition
as Yitzhak, Hedwig's lover

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:26.880
and backup singer.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.300
And I had met Mike
Potter at Kim's Video.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.110
He was one of the
famously surly clerks

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.705
at Kim's Video who's like,
"I can't rent you this.

00:08:36.705 --> 00:08:37.830
You haven't watched it yet?

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:38.669
This is a classic.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.544
Fuck you, get out."

00:08:40.544 --> 00:08:42.480
That whole thing.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.025
And I said, I'm
going to SqueezeBox.

00:08:44.025 --> 00:08:44.525
Box.

00:08:44.525 --> 00:08:45.960
He's like, "Oh, I go there.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.160
I do wigs sometimes."

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.420
I said, I need a wig done,
and he did my second wig

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:54.300
at the show at
SqueezeBox, which was--

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.850
Mike, what was it made out of?

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:01.230
Toilet paper rolls with hair
wrapped around it, staples

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:02.640
and hot glue.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.788
And it fell apart on stage.

00:09:04.788 --> 00:09:05.580
Like, the toilet --

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:06.080
Perfectly.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.140
--paper was dangling.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.460
I was like I'm fired.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.380
And then he was like,
"Can we do that again?

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.060
Can that happen every time?"

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.680
I was like, "No!

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.000
That can't happen ever again."

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.010
I mean, I was like
ready to walk out

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.110
and be like "good luck
with your character."

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.030
Because I knew I was
fired, but I wasn't.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:22.110
You've invoked Mike.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:23.580
So, Mike, hello!

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.090
Hi.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:27.540
Mike Potter is a hair and
make-up artist extraordinaire

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.450
who works in editorial,
advertising, celebrity,

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.630
and motion picture make-up,
and he's also a painter.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.430
Collaborating with
John Cameron Mitchell,

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.920
Potter created
Hedwig's iconic wigs

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.070
and make-up for the
musical and film adaptation

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.410
of Hedwig and the Angry Inch.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:44.970
So Mike, we've invoked you.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:48.180
And we stopped at
the toilet paper roll

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:52.020
wig wrapped in a hair
that fell apart hopefully

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:52.890
at the right moment.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.557
I mean, it must've
been the right moment

00:09:54.557 --> 00:09:56.340
if it was requested again.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:57.750
It kind of didn't fall apart.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.800
Well it started bad
before that, because when

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.040
we were standing at
the bathroom door

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.820
before he was going on stage
in said toilet paper wig,

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.540
he had a cup of
tea in his hand--

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:09.040
Coffee.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.750
--and someone opened
the door and the tea

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.480
flew all over his face.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.730
Hot tea.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.460
It kind of like-- it was
like the first setting spray.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:19.100
It was setting!

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:19.600
Yeah!

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.975
It was the first
setting spray ever made.

00:10:21.975 --> 00:10:22.850
Then we move forward.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.420
We were doing Fez
and places like that.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.910
And John wanted to
go more structural.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.980
So I was making,
if you can imagine,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:33.490
you know the tubes
that a dryer has?

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.790
Those plastic, metal, the
big tube that's the exhaust?

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:37.860
Oh yeah, the metal ones.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:38.020
Yeah.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:38.650
Uh-huh?

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.050
I have a picture of John--
because he decided he wanted

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:43.090
to do a side part version--

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:45.730
where that wig is
made out of those,

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.570
hair wrapped around that.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:51.010
And I just remember he snatched
his and the wig flew off.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.640
It was just a series of
unfortunate disasters

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.310
and events that kind of
worked for the character

00:10:57.310 --> 00:10:58.120
over the years.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:03.430
But, John and Stephen always
had these beautiful songs.

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:07.810
And the singing and everything
else kind of made up for it.

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:12.350
And it became OK, the tragedy
of the hair and make-up.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:12.850
To fail.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:16.420
Yeah I mean, I've always
said the only reason is

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:19.750
Hedwig's hair and makeup is
supposed to be kind of bad

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:23.995
is the way that I was successful
in the end of that process.

00:11:23.995 --> 00:11:25.720
You failed beautifully.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.620
You, RuPaul gets it right in
terms of all the skills needed

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:30.340
to do drag.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:34.010
Meaning, making your own
stuff, you're a designer,

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:36.490
you're an editor, you're
a director, a performer,

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:37.960
sometimes a songwriter.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.500
Drag and to a lesser
extent stand-up,

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:45.280
is the most fully formed
kind of performance

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:46.840
in that you have to do it all.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.270
And oftentimes you're
mixing your own damn song,

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:50.900
putting your own track on.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:51.880
Oh yeah.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:53.830
Right?

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:54.760
I learned about--

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:57.580
Hitting play on the player,
running to open the curtain,

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:01.400
and then hurry up and get back
into position for the audience.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:01.900
Yes.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.567
And they're all like,
it's so glamorous!

00:12:03.567 --> 00:12:05.650
You're like, mm-hmm.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:09.130
That gets to a thing that
we just sort of adapted.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:11.920
Hedwig always was
wherever Hedwig was.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.480
And so then if
something happened,

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.880
it wasn't John and
Stephen's performance

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.460
where stuff was going bad, it
was Hedwig and the Angry Inch's

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:19.090
performance--

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:19.395
Exactly.

00:12:19.395 --> 00:12:20.740
--where stuff was going wrong.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:22.120
And that was the reality.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:25.150
And as long as you accepted
that, it didn't matter.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:27.790
We're at the Jane
Street Theater.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.745
There would be leaks in
the bathroom upstairs,

00:12:29.745 --> 00:12:31.120
and they'd have
to bring buckets.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:32.690
Dead bodies.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:33.190
Yes!

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.390
Yeah, dead bodies.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.690
And pieces of the set would
come crashing down on us.

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:41.860
You know, hit the bass player,
and he's like bleeding.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.710
We were in a SRO
hotel which means--

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:48.290
not standing room only--
but single room occupancy,

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.110
which is like welfare hotel.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.810
And it was like German
backpackers and junkies

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:53.980
upstairs.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.110
And once the audience
was coming in

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.240
and they were taking a body out.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.730
And that was our gig.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.670
We were outside
the theater system.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:06.310
I was such a theater
queen, and Stephen

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:07.700
was such a rock and roll queen.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:12.250
So we created a third space that
was part theater, part cabaret,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.700
part rock to keep
the character honest.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:18.940
And then by the time
we got to Off-Broadway,

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.880
we opened and nobody
was interested.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:25.960
The critics really liked us,
but audiences were horrified.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:29.260
Often very silent shows
like in the movie.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.460
It was only the press
kind of making it hip.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.890
It took Glenn Close
coming 20 times.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:38.920
And the press agents making sure
that the press was always there

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:40.540
when a celebrity showed up.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.520
And then they would
pack the house

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:43.750
with people with free seats.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.828
They're like this huge
hit downtown that sold out

00:13:46.828 --> 00:13:48.370
and all these
celebrities are coming.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:49.780
It's like, then the
celebrities would go away

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.363
and the press would leave and
we performed for like 20 people.

00:13:52.363 --> 00:13:54.880
It was totally fake.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.320
But then it would be
like Marilyn Manson

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.530
and Barry Manilow
were there one night.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.000
Elliott Smith and Madonna.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.080
Patti LuPone.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:04.600
You know, it was just
like this mix was crazy.

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:08.140
Pee Wee Herman, David Bowie,
and Bea Arthur and Lou Reed--

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:09.760
And Laurie Anderson.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:10.690
Pete Burns.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.000
It was out of control.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.190
We were overwhelmed
that our heroes

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:19.270
were here and approving of us.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.700
That drag legends, too,
were approving of it.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:25.060
Because I still
felt like a fake.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:28.310
I hadn't paid my dues
as a drag person.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:32.650
So even though we were limping
along and never really a hit,

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.140
we stuck it in there
and suddenly there

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:36.760
was a bidding war for the film.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:38.410
OK, that is perfect timing.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:41.740
You've already
invoked her, but I

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.780
need to introduce her properly.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.240
She is an actor and
activist who starred in

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.160
film, theater, and television.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.380
And, of course, she
starred as Yitzhak

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:53.500
in the Off-Broadway musical
Hedwig And The Angry Inch,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:56.530
and then later reprised
the role in the film.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:59.715
Please help me
welcome Miriam Shor.

00:14:59.715 --> 00:15:01.020
Hey, Miriam!

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.198
I am excited to be here.

00:15:03.198 --> 00:15:04.740
It's so nice to meet
you, Peppermint.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.700
I'm just a huge fan.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:07.200
I know.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.500
You're one of the people
that I haven't actually met.

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:10.740
In person.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.460
I'm over here kind of like
sweating and fangirling.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.090
It's so great to be here.

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:17.970
It's so fun to think
back to all that time.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.310
I was just remembering--

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:25.470
I can remember so vividly
meeting Stephen and John.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:29.520
It was the first
audition in New York City

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.650
that a person ever sent me on.

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:32.965
Was for the workshop.

00:15:32.965 --> 00:15:33.720
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.970
What do you mean, that a
person ever sent you on?

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:37.290
I met with an agent.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.343
I was just a kid, and
I met with an agent.

00:15:39.343 --> 00:15:41.010
And they were like,
well, we don't know.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:41.700
Maybe.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.158
We'll send you out
on a few things.

00:15:43.158 --> 00:15:44.052
We'll see how you do.

00:15:44.052 --> 00:15:45.510
And the first thing
they sent me on

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.840
was to audition for a
workshop version of Hedwig.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.820
It wasn't even fully a play yet.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:51.630
So I was like, "Oh!

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:52.630
This is amazing.

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:53.670
I mean, this is so--

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:54.840
what is this?"

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:59.250
And I have a piece of paper that
I wrote all the notes down on.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.760
They were like, I wrote
Hedgewick and the Angry Itch.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.620
And they were like, Yitzhak
is a man played by a woman.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.010
Former drag queen
named "Krystal Nacht."

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.800
And they were like, you
need to bring a song in.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.990
What did you sing?

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:18.090
Well here's the thing.

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:19.470
And this is kind of magical.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:23.680
So when we did the workshop,
the last song in the show

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.140
was not "Midnight Radio" yet.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:28.650
It was John as Hedwig
singing "You Light Up

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:30.720
My Life" in German.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.170
And I have no idea why,
I was not asked to,

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:38.020
but to my audition-- and I had
never sung this song before.

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:39.600
I don't know why.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.110
I sang "You Light Up My Life."

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:41.610
Lies.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:42.110
Lies!

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:42.990
I swear to God.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:43.770
For real?

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.030
I'm not lying.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.710
I don't know why.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:49.520
Why on Earth would
you pick that song?

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:50.860
I don't know!

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:51.510
Wait, John.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:53.700
Were you at the audition, John?

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:54.570
Yeah.

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:59.370
It must have stuck in my
brain because later that was

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:01.410
sort of our temporary finale.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:03.840
I feel like when I met
John and Stephen and Peter,

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:07.470
I just sort of was like, I kind
of feel like I met my people.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:10.111
And I kind of
understand this person,

00:17:10.111 --> 00:17:11.819
even though he's not
really a person yet.

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:14.849
You were so good at kind
of explaining his pain.

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:19.319
But somehow, I just tapped
into that rage and that sadness

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:20.069
that is Yitzhak.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:21.810
And it just felt
weirdly familiar.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:25.349
And I just was excited
to go on that trip.

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:26.609
We had no idea at that point.

00:17:26.609 --> 00:17:28.079
We weren't Off-Broadway yet.

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:29.700
It was still very new.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.250
And you guys were like, we don't
really know if she can sing,

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.020
but we kind of like her.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.030
I take credit for that.

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:39.720
Because we each had names
written on our piece of paper.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.420
And we, like, turned them
over, Peter and John and me.

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:44.040
And Miriam was our first choice.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:46.020
And we're like, I don't
know if she sings,

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:47.700
but she just seems
like one of us.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:48.660
We like her.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:49.380
[LAUGHS]

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:54.570
Miriam also taught me a weird
thing that happens with actors.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.340
You can do things if
your character can

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.520
that you can't in your life.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.640
And Miriam hadn't really
sung rock and roll,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:05.910
but the character had
to scream rock and roll.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.890
And suddenly because the
character had to, she could.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.620
And I find the
same thing with me.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.310
I can like catch a ball really
easy if I'm in character,

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.860
but in real life
I'm like Oh my God!

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:18.030
You know?

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:19.620
The world of musical
theater has--

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.480
I just always felt kind of--

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.060
I always felt like I was
trying to fit into that world

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:27.360
because I just felt like I'm
clearly much more of a freak

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.100
than is allowed in that world.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:30.570
And I just felt
right at home when

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.450
I met you and John and Stephen.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.570
I just felt like mm-hmm.

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:35.070
Yeah.

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:38.140
Interesting thing with the
flow of gender in the show,

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:41.220
which is throughout,
is originally

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.080
that character was
more of a factotum.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.240
Someone helping out with props.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.900
Craig Chester played a
version of that named Georg.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.760
And when we were thinking
of the real show,

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.370
Stephen said it would be
great if that character could

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:55.080
be a backup singer
and I would love

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.400
to hear high registered voice.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:01.350
So I was like, let's
have a person born female

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:06.180
with a high voice or
available high notes

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:10.180
playing a man who wants
to dress as a woman.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:18.330
So in effect, an AFAB actor
playing a man who wants to do

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.550
drag.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.300
And it was perfect
for the piece.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:27.090
Because we want the
audience throughout to be,

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:31.470
not confused, but aware
that the world is fluid.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:34.560
We are all-- we all have these
energies that sometimes we

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:38.700
call male and female in us
and we can pull them out

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:39.840
when needed.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.470
Others are more natural.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:44.950
But some are hidden, and if
you don't let them come out

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.490
it's like an animal
that dies in the wall.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.700
It can really
stink the place up.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:53.910
We were always
aware of the flow.

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:55.920
Hedwig was a boy
named Hansel who

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:01.540
was comfortable in his
gender and assigned a gender

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:04.180
by, in effect, the
patriarchy, by his boyfriend,

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:09.670
and by the government, and
is left alone and crushed

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:10.660
after that.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:14.290
And it's through drag that,
Peppermint reminded me,

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:16.690
is where the
empowerment happens.

00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:18.850
Not necessarily through
a trans journey,

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:21.310
because it was more
of a victimization.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:26.380
But taking what you've got,
using drag, rock 'n roll,

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:31.990
performance, to find yourself,
to create yourself, as Hedwig,

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:33.110
is the journey.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:35.330
And a lot of people
can relate to that.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:40.180
I get a lot, especially
transmasculine people, say,

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.790
I love the flow
of gender in this

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:45.460
because as a
transmasculine person,

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:47.660
I want to be able
to do female drag.

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:51.670
I don't want to be
limited by anyone.

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.040
I want freedom.

00:20:54.040 --> 00:21:00.160
And Hedwig's journey to
freedom is a lot of obstacles.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.350
And, hopefully,
there's fewer obstacles

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:06.610
in real life for others,
but the character

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:10.480
allowed a little space
that all kinds of people

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:13.480
with different kinds of
backgrounds and genders

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:15.730
found the space to be within.

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:18.280
And we love that.

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:20.680
I mean, I will say,
too, playing Yitzhak,

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:26.410
it was such a gift to get
to explore what being a man

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:30.550
means to me as a person.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:34.540
I got to just explore that
every night, and not just that,

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:37.840
but what a man who wants
to be a drag queen.

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:40.360
So I got to really
go on a journey that

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:43.420
was not afforded people.

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.370
This was the '90s.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:48.310
That wasn't-- I mean, it's still
a struggle for a lot of people.

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.010
And it felt like such a gift.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:55.030
The point was just to explore
it and share it with everybody

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.280
watching in that moment.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:01.070
It was kind of
extraordinary for all of us.

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:02.140
And you could feel it.

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:06.610
It was so visceral in
the theater sometimes.

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:08.230
It was just really electric.

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:12.400
And I remember when we
thought that maybe it

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.280
was going to be a
movie, too, which

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.030
just seemed extraordinary,
because we were

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:19.180
such underdogs for so long.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:20.980
Like we always joked,
like, oh, yeah,

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.332
we're going to be on Broadway
and like 178th Street.

00:22:23.332 --> 00:22:24.790
It's never going
to be on Broadway.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:26.080
Let's not kid ourselves.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:28.127
When they talked about
this could be a film,

00:22:28.127 --> 00:22:29.710
I remember thinking,
how are you going

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:33.730
to translate that
intersection of excitement

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:37.480
and fear and transgression
and acceptance

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.210
that the play was able to do.

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.072
So that was kind of
an exciting journey.

00:22:42.072 --> 00:22:44.530
Like I said, it was my first
freaking audition in New York,

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:46.240
so I was like, oh,
is this how art goes?

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:52.540
Miriam, you mentioned a bit of
the journey from the workshop

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:55.090
to Off-Broadway to the movie.

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:57.460
I'm guessing there was
several challenges.

00:22:57.460 --> 00:23:01.900
But one of the big changes
was, of course, with costumes.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:05.770
And so I'd like to introduce
this next person, who

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:09.070
is one of the most unique and
creative forces in the fashion

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.900
and entertainment industries.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:13.930
A multi-dimensional
artist, visual artist,

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:17.230
who brings her exemplary vision
to film, fashion, theater,

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:20.660
opera, music, and media!

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:21.710
Ah!

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:23.250
I love her so much.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.550
Please help me welcome
Arianne Phillips.

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:26.700
Hey, Arianne!

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:29.120
Thanks, Peppermint.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:30.260
So happy to be here.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:34.370
I'd love to hear your
experiences from once you first

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:35.900
got on board.

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:37.400
How did it begin?

00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:40.340
It's very bittersweet,
but I came to Hedwig

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:42.620
through our
incredible production

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:49.340
designer, Therese DePrez,
who's not with us anymore.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:51.980
We had worked on
a film together,

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:56.810
and she recommended me to John.

00:23:56.810 --> 00:24:00.920
And I got the script
for the movie.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:05.060
And I, honestly, didn't
know what I was reading.

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:07.670
I was like I don't know
what the hell this is,

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:10.220
but it's fucking genius.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:13.170
There was animation and puppets.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:18.210
And not your typical
camera angle, panning left,

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:19.730
panning right, dolly moves.

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:22.280
There is incredible
character development.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:25.280
And I didn't hear the music
because I hadn't seen the show.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:26.360
I had heard about it.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:27.200
I got the script.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:27.900
I read it.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:28.760
I just knew--

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.860
I read it a couple of
times because I really

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:33.450
couldn't figure out
which way was up or down.

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:36.650
But I got the emotion
and the heart.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.080
I trusted Therese implicitly.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.760
And then I went to--

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:43.400
I think it was
the Avalon Hotel--

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.110
Yep.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:47.060
I met Mike and John
at the same time.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:48.620
I was in the pool.

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:51.590
Yeah, Mike, you were in the
pool, the kidney-shaped pool.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:53.660
Immediately, I can
really echo what

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:57.320
Miriam said that I felt
like these are my people.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:01.130
Working in this industry,
we are thrown together

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:03.560
with so many different
types of people.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.540
That's the brilliance.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.550
Different backgrounds,
different aesthetics.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:11.360
And being a theater
nerd as a kid,

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:16.520
I immediately just
felt seen in a way

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:20.240
just by reading the script and
fell in love with John and Mike

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.940
at the same time.

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:26.270
And will forever be
grateful to Therese DePrez

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:30.560
for bringing me in, and for
John taking a shot on me.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.360
I went to New York
shortly thereafter

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:34.310
and saw the show on Jane Street.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:38.810
I think the first show I saw
was with Ally Sheedy, actually.

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.750
And I remember John,
you saying, you've

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:45.750
got to see it with
someone else, as well.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:48.080
So that was how I
came to the film.

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:53.900
And 20 years later,
some of my family,

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:56.510
closest, we've had
the pleasure of going

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.240
through this
unprecedented journey.

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:00.410
And Hedwig is always--

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:03.710
how I explain it--

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:05.870
Hedwig is my Rocky
Horror Picture Show.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:10.580
When I saw Rocky Horror
Picture Show as a kid,

00:26:10.580 --> 00:26:14.690
it was so provocative and it
was about a similar message

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:17.780
in terms of don't
dream it, be it.

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:24.320
Finding your true identity and
using the artifice of sparkles

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:27.740
and hair and make-up
to express yourself

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:31.490
and everything in between
and so much heart.

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:37.160
I feel so privileged to
be part of this family.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:39.830
It's a connection I
have with strangers,

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:41.390
whether they find
me on the internet

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.320
or hear about it on set.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.260
It's multi-generational.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:50.120
It crosses so many
cultures and ages.

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:51.260
There's no boundary.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.270
And my grandmother,
who isn't with us

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:55.760
anymore, I remember
when she saw the film,

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.430
she was so blown away by it.

00:26:58.430 --> 00:27:02.600
And that's the power
of beautiful story

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:06.710
and compelling music and
brilliant performance.

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:10.100
And just doing this
film has turned out

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:14.030
to be the most representative of
who I am as an artist in terms

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:18.620
of my creativity and
I just really felt

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:22.460
encouraged by everyone,
John and Stephen and Frank's

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:23.390
beautiful work, and

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:28.620
Mike endlessly-- my costumes
are completed by his work

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:31.100
and were inspired by his work.

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:34.100
There's not a beginning and
an end like most projects.

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:38.000
We all are so connected
and the performers.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:40.550
So that's my monologue.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:42.140
Okay, this is perfect timing.

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:46.490
It's time for me to bring in
our next genius who grew up

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:48.560
in Baltimore, Maryland--
right near where

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:51.020
I grew up-- and
studied classical piano

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:52.460
into his late teens.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:54.860
While learning Italian
in Florence, Italy,

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.680
he had the opportunity to
work on a TV commercial there

00:27:57.680 --> 00:27:59.930
and was immediately
drawn to filmmaking

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:03.020
and now works in features,
TV shows, commercials,

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:06.080
documentaries, industrials,
webisodes, art films,

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:06.900
music videos.

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:08.600
You name it, he has done it.

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.810
Of course, I'm talking
about Frankie DeMarco.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:16.220
Okay, Frankie, please tell
us how you came onboard.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:17.600
Yeah.

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:19.880
I came through the
Sundance Film Lab,

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:23.810
through Michelle Satter, who
was running a Sundance Summer

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:24.710
Directors' Lab.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:26.390
Michelle was looking for DPs.

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:28.130
I think she had already
talked with John.

00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:30.350
And just based on the
timbre of my voice

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:32.812
and probably the timbre of
John's voice, she thought,

00:28:32.812 --> 00:28:34.770
these two would probably
get along pretty well.

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.010
So I went and did the Lab.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.980
To Arianne's point, about
there's the standard way

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:42.650
to shoot everything-- when
we were doing the Lab,

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:46.697
I remember we had these sets
that were just so overused.

00:28:46.697 --> 00:28:48.530
And I said, why don't
we flip the set around

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:50.363
so we're looking at the
back of the set that

00:28:50.363 --> 00:28:53.810
has the exposed wood.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:56.900
I think reading the script
and seeing how John works.

00:28:56.900 --> 00:28:59.990
He's willing to fail and try
again and fail and try again,

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:02.390
and that's always sort
of been my mantra, too --

00:29:02.390 --> 00:29:05.750
I'm willing to fail, let's try
it, and John encouraged that.

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:09.230
So we got along very well
at the Lab and time passed.

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:11.360
Then we ended up working
at John's apartment--

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:14.000
John and I lived
nearby at the time.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:16.880
It would most of the time
be John and Therese DePrez,

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:18.380
the production
designer, and myself,

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:22.040
and we went through the
script page by page by page

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:24.950
and picked the script apart
and talked about things.

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:26.930
Threw ideas out.

00:29:26.930 --> 00:29:30.320
John cherry picked the best
ideas and made changes.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:32.750
John, how did the movie
come about and tell me

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:35.270
about the challenges of
being a first-time director.

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:38.330
Well, it was a strange
situation, like I mentioned.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:41.630
Stephen Trask had connections
with New Line Cinema,

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:47.000
so did I. Bob Shaye who
ran New Line was really

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:48.590
my godfather with this.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:52.250
And Bob directed me in
the movie Book of Love.

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:53.700
And in my audition--

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:56.390
I hadn't read the script
till I got in the office--

00:29:56.390 --> 00:29:57.690
and he said, what do you think?

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:59.450
And I said my
character is really

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:02.870
written very homophobically,
Bob, I have to be honest.

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:06.260
This was like in '88 and people
didn't really say those things.

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:10.430
And he's like, well,
it's homoerotic.

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:16.930
I'm like, well, homogeneous
begins with homo, as well.

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:19.490
They all mean different things.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:22.720
So the characters are very--

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.790
every negative stereotype.

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:27.760
And he said, oh, I
didn't think of that.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:30.190
So how would you do it
if it was rewritten?

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:31.660
And he eventually rewrote it.

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:34.630
But years later,
he came to the show

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:37.330
on the behest of Mike
De Luca, and there

00:30:37.330 --> 00:30:38.650
were tears in his eyes after.

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:42.850
And he said, you were
very straight with me

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:46.450
in a creative, engaging
way with Book of Love

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:48.410
and I appreciated that.

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:50.050
And I'd like to help you--

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:51.700
I'd like to finance this film.

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:55.000
And they were supportive of
me directing it and starring

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.440
in it, which
wouldn't happen now.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:59.890
It would have to
be some big star.

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:02.330
And suddenly we
were the hot thing.

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:04.690
So everyone would show up.

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:07.810
Danny DeVito really
wanted to produce it.

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:10.270
Forest Whitaker
wanted to direct.

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:12.730
I said to Forest, I actually
would like to direct.

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:14.860
He said, John, that would
be a terrible mistake.

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:20.223
And I was like, Forest,
I hardly know you.

00:31:20.223 --> 00:31:21.140
Is that what you said?

00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:22.070
Forest--

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:23.490
That's what she said.

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:24.947
I mean, I hadn't
directed before.

00:31:24.947 --> 00:31:26.030
So it was a bit of a risk.

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:31.430
I directed it on stage, so it
was a strange moment when--

00:31:31.430 --> 00:31:34.250
independent film,
it's not hip anymore.

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:35.690
It's not part of the zeitgeist.

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:36.740
It was in the '90s.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.740
In the 2000s there was
numbers of companies

00:31:39.740 --> 00:31:42.230
who were financing, and
we had a bidding war

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.990
amongst a number of companies,
even though we were not

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:47.990
a big hit financially,
we were a critical hit.

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:49.830
And we had an opportunity.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:52.280
So we knew we really wanted
to work with New Line

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:57.050
and eventually we brought in
Killer Films, Christine Vachon,

00:31:57.050 --> 00:32:01.010
who's my hero, who worked
with Todd Haynes and all

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:02.130
of these great people.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.650
We also had a good
year to work on it.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:07.250
So I went to the Lab, met Frank.

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:11.480
I remember the first time
I directed a scene on film

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:12.470
it didn't go that well.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:16.470
The second one, Michelle said
to me, you're a filmmaker now.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:17.940
And I started crying.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:19.440
And it was a
beautiful experience.

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:21.320
And I trusted Frank.

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:24.140
Even though he didn't
have the credits

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:26.210
necessarily that
New Line wanted,

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:28.220
I had a great vibe with him.

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:32.300
And I said no to all
these more famous DPs.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:36.500
And that was the right
decision because Frank DeMarco

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.230
and Andrew Marcus, the
editor, in many ways

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:40.610
were co-directors for me.

00:32:40.610 --> 00:32:42.410
They were much more experienced.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:44.920
Andrew had worked
with Merchant Ivory,

00:32:44.920 --> 00:32:49.370
Room with a View, Howard's
End, Remains of the Day.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:53.720
And Frank was this gutsy,
scrappy, New York-based guy

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:56.300
coming up from the pit.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:58.700
And it was a perfect mix.

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:02.750
And Stephen had these
connections with New Line, too.

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:07.310
So there was a kind of family
vibe with New Line where

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:08.630
we felt safe.

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:11.600
Do you think that made you feel
more safe to direct yourself?

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:13.970
Because I think that sounds
like the ultimate nightmare.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:16.580
That was very scary.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.190
In some ways doing
the show I was

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:21.620
kind of directing
myself then, too,

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:24.620
and never being able to see
it because I was on stage.

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:29.150
So I was used to kind of
directing, but it was--

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:31.190
and I knew what I loved.

00:33:31.190 --> 00:33:35.570
I knew I loved Altman and
Cassavetes and Bob Fosse.

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:39.620
And All That Jazz in some
ways was the most influential

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:40.520
on this.

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:41.690
The idea that each scene--

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:42.360
The movie?

00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:42.860
Yeah.

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:45.230
The fact that each scene
could have a different style.

00:33:45.230 --> 00:33:48.020
And if there was
a strong narrator

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:51.230
you could hold those
different styles together.

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:55.700
So it was films like
that and Nashville

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.670
that I was thinking
about for this.

00:33:58.670 --> 00:34:00.020
A lot of '70s stuff.

00:34:00.020 --> 00:34:01.970
And I like the fact
that even kids today

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:03.800
can't tell when this was made.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:05.180
They're like, '70s?

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:07.460
'80s?

00:34:07.460 --> 00:34:09.440
It's out of time, which I like.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:12.230
And Frank and I made
sure we shot it on film,

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:13.409
and that was a big deal.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:15.739
So we had an unusual
situation where

00:34:15.739 --> 00:34:20.360
we had financing, distribution,
before we even shot.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:24.860
And we had a good year
to develop it and go

00:34:24.860 --> 00:34:27.170
through every scene,
Frank and I. There

00:34:27.170 --> 00:34:30.350
would be no way we could shoot
it if we didn't have that year.

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.150
I didn't have enough experience.

00:34:32.150 --> 00:34:34.250
We bit off way more
than we could chew.

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:36.500
I'm off over there
putting a wig on

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:39.980
while Frank is
shooting the grip's ass

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:42.650
as a substitute ass for myself.

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:45.800
And Andrew, the editor,
is shooting gummy bears

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:46.550
in close-up.

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:49.429
And I'm like, it's
just like SqueezeBox!

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:51.260
We're just barely
getting it together.

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:53.989
We organized what was
called the insurgency.

00:34:53.989 --> 00:34:58.280
Arianne, Mike, and Frank
stole costumes and make-up.

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:01.820
Frank stole film stock
and had his own camera,

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:05.570
and we shot some scenes
in my apartment in Toronto

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:07.460
on a weekend illegally.

00:35:07.460 --> 00:35:12.170
And shot stuff without
sound with Mike Pitt and me

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:13.290
that ended up in the film.

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:15.140
And we would never
have got those things

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:17.450
if we hadn't broken
all the rules.

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:20.540
Even the crew was overwhelmed
with our rock 'n roll style.

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:22.280
I remember the AD was just--

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:25.220
he would just, he started
crying and we ended up

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:27.570
having to assistant
direct it, too.

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:28.820
I was like, move those trucks!

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:32.030
We had Drag Day and
all those Canadians,

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:33.710
they wouldn't even take my help.

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:36.700
Ari, you and I were like
the least drag out of all.

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:39.500
The AD showed up in like tutus.

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:42.710
You had these people who I think
until this film would never--

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:45.680
it would not have occurred
to them to think about drag.

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:46.820
Teamsters and drag.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:49.190
And it was a day where
everyone was in drag

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:50.720
and that's how we shot that day.

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:53.870
That would be my ultimate fan--
that's how I see the world.

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:55.160
Everyone is in drag.

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:59.120
Put your frame of mind there
to be a drag performer.

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:00.350
Even in the smallest way.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:02.300
Like, it just opens
something for you.

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:05.840
And everyone on set is
doing that, it's just

00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:07.220
going to open something up.

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:10.558
And it was such a scrappy
sense on that film,

00:36:10.558 --> 00:36:12.350
very much like what
you were talking about,

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:13.975
Stephen and John,
when you were talking

00:36:13.975 --> 00:36:15.270
about being Off-Broadway.

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.170
The way that Hedwig
was Off-Broadway

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:20.170
was not like we had to fake it.

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:23.020
It was whatever happened
was what happened.

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:26.210
And being on that stage was
reacting to whatever happened.

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:30.130
I've never felt more alive in
the moment of a theater piece.

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:33.490
And that was very much
translated to the screen.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.410
I was going to say we
lost one of the locations

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:39.070
and then we just had to
shoot that tire scene.

00:36:39.070 --> 00:36:39.730
Remember that?

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:41.920
And it was like, that's right.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:44.560
That feels right for
Hedwig, that you're scrappy

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:47.500
and that you have to make
do with what you have

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.300
and that it actually
is beautiful.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:52.360
The beauty of the
piece, too, is that it

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:55.300
evolves around kind of
the circumstance you're

00:36:55.300 --> 00:37:00.430
in that day when you're
performing or shooting.

00:37:00.430 --> 00:37:01.990
It lives on.

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:04.960
Hedwig has her own
energy, and that's

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:07.510
why it's authentic at any time.

00:37:07.510 --> 00:37:12.820
Well, Hedwig also only had two
costumes in the original stage

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:13.660
production.

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:16.130
But in the film she had 41.

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:18.070
So can you talk about that?

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:21.430
And how you would
go from two to 41?

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:26.290
Well, the first thing I said to
Ari, whose earlier work blew me

00:37:26.290 --> 00:37:27.110
away--

00:37:27.110 --> 00:37:29.920
I said, Ari, Hedwig's
costumes have

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:33.160
to be creative,
unique, and affordable.

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:36.220
Because she is working
out of thrift shops.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:38.590
And Ari is like, done.

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:42.280
She brought in stuff
from earlier movies

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:46.790
she'd worked on or abandoned
or rock 'n roll music stylist

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:47.290
stuff.

00:37:47.290 --> 00:37:50.590
I was wearing Britney
Spears' skirt.

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:53.210
I was wearing
Cameron Diaz's shirt.

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:56.690
It was like she was a
hermit crab and a scavenger.

00:37:56.690 --> 00:37:57.500
Right, Ari?

00:37:57.500 --> 00:37:58.000
Well.

00:37:58.000 --> 00:37:58.500
Yeah.

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:01.180
I mean, what I would say
it's like method costuming.

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:04.450
Getting into the character
doesn't stop with the actors.

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:06.940
It's really like-- I
always say that costumes

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:09.925
are a beam-me-up suit for
an actor to access a time

00:38:09.925 --> 00:38:11.050
and place in the character.

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:14.320
And I take that seriously.

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:15.870
Unfortunately for John--

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:17.460
It was torturous.

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:19.150
It was torture.

00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:24.610
Mike Potter and I cutting the
hair dress and Mike and John

00:38:24.610 --> 00:38:29.410
crying, literally, on a
Saturday in our hotel because--

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:31.570
I shaved his eyebrows off.

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:32.445
Yeah.

00:38:32.445 --> 00:38:34.570
Which still haven't recovered!

00:38:34.570 --> 00:38:37.480
Except RevitaBrow,
Peppermint, that we all love.

00:38:37.480 --> 00:38:41.710
Ari, remember we went to the
Valley and we went to the

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:44.620
By The Pound stores,
and you got some really

00:38:44.620 --> 00:38:46.780
great-- like that mall costume.

00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:50.590
Like the Rickie Lee Jones,
like pink with the beret.

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:52.900
It was like Ari had all
these great resources that

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:55.330
was high and low.

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:56.200
Goodwill.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:56.830
Badwill.

00:38:56.830 --> 00:38:57.550
High, low.

00:38:57.550 --> 00:38:58.970
But, like, a lot of low.

00:38:58.970 --> 00:39:02.830
But Ari can always make
it beautiful somehow.

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:05.290
John, you tasked me with that.

00:39:05.290 --> 00:39:09.400
And that was just the
kind of meaning that I

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:10.780
could put into those costumes.

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:14.500
And it was doable
and really like fun

00:39:14.500 --> 00:39:16.990
and ripping them apart and
putting together as Hedwig

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:17.710
would.

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:20.320
And, actually, I think that
has informed me in my work

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:21.040
ever since.

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:25.600
To try and approach
the costumes as if--

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:28.150
how a character
dresses themselves.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:33.040
And Hedwig was just the riches
and gifts that kept on giving.

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:36.670
Mike, can you tell us about her
hair and her look in the movie?

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:38.560
30 wigs.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:42.050
That sounds like a fantasy and a
nightmare all at the same time.

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:44.192
So I went in, and the
studio was, like, well

00:39:44.192 --> 00:39:46.150
we're going to have you
oversee, and you're not

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:47.110
going to do John.

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:48.850
Like, we have other people--

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:49.570
union people.

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:52.210
And I'm like, I'm the only
person that's touching John.

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:53.950
I didn't have an assistant.

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:56.410
I had to do some of
those wigs in triplicate.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:59.020
John never wanted
to wear a bobby pin,

00:39:59.020 --> 00:40:01.750
so they would fly off his head
and it would be like, cut.

00:40:01.750 --> 00:40:02.920
The wig flew off.

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:06.910
But I just remember a lot
of Arianne teaching me.

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:09.250
It was my first movie
I ever did was Hedwig.

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:12.670
I think Arianne was really
a guiding force for me.

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:14.770
And John let us,
at a certain point,

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:16.250
kind of do what we wanted.

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:16.750
And I did.

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:21.220
I came from that all drag Don
Hill's world where you threw

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:23.320
together a bunch of things.

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:26.680
And luckily Ari had things
a little more planned out.

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:29.320
But I just remember we
had planned out wigs.

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:31.990
Well, we had looks planned out
and then at the last minute

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:33.340
they would just change.

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:37.150
Or Ari would be like,
this is all I got--

00:40:37.150 --> 00:40:38.260
these glitter shoes.

00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:41.290
And I'm like, okay, I'm going
to do a lip to match the shoes.

00:40:41.290 --> 00:40:43.880
Not that you would
ever see them together.

00:40:43.880 --> 00:40:46.300
But we tried to
connect and I think

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:51.370
having the freedom to be allowed
to do what we wanted to do.

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:54.130
And I come from kind of
sort of like a little bit

00:40:54.130 --> 00:40:55.820
of a trashy background.

00:40:55.820 --> 00:40:58.990
He comes from like Sharpie
in your dark roots.

00:40:58.990 --> 00:40:59.635
That's you.

00:40:59.635 --> 00:40:59.900
Oh.

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:00.130
Yeah.

00:41:00.130 --> 00:41:00.630
Yeah.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:03.250
I give all my cred to Arianne.

00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:06.250
I mean I always tell people
she taught me everything

00:41:06.250 --> 00:41:08.602
I know about working on a film.

00:41:08.602 --> 00:41:11.185
Can we talk a little bit about
the production designer Therese

00:41:11.185 --> 00:41:12.250
DePrez?

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:14.950
Therese, in some ways, was--

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:18.250
I mean Ari, too-- but
Therese in some ways

00:41:18.250 --> 00:41:21.260
had worked longer in
film than any of us.

00:41:21.260 --> 00:41:24.200
And I knew her from
the Killer Films days.

00:41:24.200 --> 00:41:30.830
She did a film called Dotty
Gets Spanked with Todd Haynes.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:32.990
She was the hip,
New York person.

00:41:32.990 --> 00:41:35.420
She was seven feet tall.

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:36.920
She had the best
taste in the world.

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:40.745
Her apartment was just full
of shit that was beautiful.

00:41:40.745 --> 00:41:43.460
Her wedding was the most
designed wedding of all time.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:45.080
I sang "Origin of Love."

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:48.500
And she was pure.

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:51.650
She was pure New York cool.

00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:53.510
Hip without the attitude.

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:55.250
Just sweetie.

00:41:55.250 --> 00:41:57.475
She came in and brought
in Ari because they

00:41:57.475 --> 00:41:58.590
had been working together.

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:01.070
I brought in Mike and Frank --

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:03.860
and Andrew was new to all of us.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:07.160
And Stephen, of course, is
bringing in his musical people

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:08.510
from different angles, too.

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:11.060
So a lot of people who
didn't have film experience.

00:42:11.060 --> 00:42:16.850
But we had grand old Therese,
who did a very difficult shoot.

00:42:16.850 --> 00:42:20.180
I'm not sure how-- it was like,
really hard on her physically.

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:20.915
She never slept.

00:42:20.915 --> 00:42:21.900
She never slept.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:22.640
Magician.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.390
She never slept.

00:42:23.390 --> 00:42:24.740
Every detail.

00:42:24.740 --> 00:42:28.640
Bilgewater's restaurant,
the worst seafood restaurant

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:32.930
in the world with decorations
of sinking ships on the wall.

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:36.110
It was a sinking
ship-themed TGI Friday's.

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:36.890
Detail.

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:39.440
Layers and layers
of story and detail.

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:42.770
Like the oven in the
German apartment and--

00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:44.970
Oh my God, there was so much.

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:46.900
It was more than
we could afford.

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:47.870
Not afford.

00:42:47.870 --> 00:42:49.640
More than we were worth.

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:52.080
We had tons of references.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:56.600
So we were all in the same
world when it was time to shoot.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:59.033
Because usually films
people don't work

00:42:59.033 --> 00:43:00.200
unless they're on the clock.

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:04.010
And we were all working
for nothing early on.

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:06.110
Usually it's like three
months before, right?

00:43:06.110 --> 00:43:09.530
And that's why things look
half-assed these days.

00:43:09.530 --> 00:43:14.150
But this looked considered,
thought through, fun, varied.

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:18.080
And I was learning from them
as I only knew what I liked.

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:20.150
I didn't know exactly
how to do anything.

00:43:20.150 --> 00:43:23.240
I want to ask the whole
panel about the movie

00:43:23.240 --> 00:43:26.240
and how cinematic it was with
the bouncing ball, animations

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:28.580
and just all the things that
were done with the split

00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:31.910
screens that you couldn't
do in the stage version.

00:43:31.910 --> 00:43:34.133
I'd like just free for all it.

00:43:34.133 --> 00:43:35.300
No, I was just going to say.

00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:40.130
I mean because we were
shooting a live-action movie,

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:44.360
no longer a stage play on one
single set with no set changes.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:47.990
And we had to work with
an animator, Emily.

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:49.400
Emily Hubley.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:51.920
We also had a lot of
visual transitions

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:55.550
because we would go from
live-action to animation back

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:58.010
to live-action.

00:43:58.010 --> 00:44:02.700
You have to film in such a
way that this cuts together.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:06.410
So I think literally, we had
a year of free preparation

00:44:06.410 --> 00:44:07.910
meeting at each
other's apartments.

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:11.060
It really helped because the
movie has a ton of transitions,

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:13.820
and I think it's part of the
charm and the fun of the movie.

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.910
To go from East
Berlin suddenly bang

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:19.310
we're into the next
place or whatever.

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:20.930
Animation or another new set.

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:23.790
That question I asked before,
which I kept asking myself,

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:25.940
which was like, how are
we going to translate

00:44:25.940 --> 00:44:31.167
this visceral energy of
doing live theater way over

00:44:31.167 --> 00:44:32.750
on the West Side
Highway when that was

00:44:32.750 --> 00:44:35.840
a place people were unsure of.

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:37.610
It wasn't a cool, hip place.

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:41.630
There was a sense of subversion
and danger in that play.

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:43.045
And we are all in it together.

00:44:43.045 --> 00:44:44.420
And I was like,
how are you going

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:47.570
to translate that into a movie?

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:49.460
We all going to be on
this ride together.

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:52.610
How are we going to feel like
it's dangerous, yet inclusive,

00:44:52.610 --> 00:44:55.640
and subversive yet home.

00:44:55.640 --> 00:44:57.540
How is that going
to come together?

00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:00.980
And I feel like everyone
put so much of themselves

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:03.500
into it when I watched it,
I was like, oh that's how.

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:05.780
You're going to use
everything you can use in film

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:08.360
and throw out a rule book
that tells you can't do that

00:45:08.360 --> 00:45:11.330
in film, and make sure
the glitter looks exactly

00:45:11.330 --> 00:45:12.980
right, which Frank would do.

00:45:12.980 --> 00:45:15.410
What film will make this
glitter look perfect?

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:19.250
Or this particular undergarment
that no one will ever see,

00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:20.810
Arianne is going
to make perfect.

00:45:20.810 --> 00:45:23.150
That immediacy translated.

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:26.450
And that alchemy that's in
a stage piece that works

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:29.270
was there in the movie,
and it's so rare.

00:45:29.270 --> 00:45:31.370
Because it's almost unnamable.

00:45:31.370 --> 00:45:34.040
But, I mean, it has to do
with the heart and the work

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:38.630
and the artistry that gets
obsessively put into it.

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:40.522
And we were away from home.

00:45:40.522 --> 00:45:41.480
We were all in Toronto.

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:43.280
We were in a different country.

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:44.960
I don't know how
many days we had,

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:47.000
but we certainly didn't
have enough days.

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:52.970
That energy of getting that
done and not losing a scene.

00:45:52.970 --> 00:45:59.360
Stuff like, for me, in post
seeing John as Hedwig flying

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:03.950
over Bilgewater's and Emily's
animation and all those tasty

00:46:03.950 --> 00:46:10.760
special vision is what created
that other dimension of magic.

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:14.090
There is a magical
reality in Hedwig

00:46:14.090 --> 00:46:18.920
that is grounded in
this soulful heart story

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:21.590
about being true to ourselves.

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:22.880
And the alchemy.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:24.585
Exactly what you said, Miriam.

00:46:24.585 --> 00:46:25.460
Do you guys remember?

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:29.390
Is the Wig in a
Box-Brady Bunch look, was

00:46:29.390 --> 00:46:32.730
that one of the
last scenes we shot?

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:36.980
I think it was and it was
like we'd been up all night.

00:46:36.980 --> 00:46:38.220
We were laughing during that.

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:40.830
We all got the giggles.

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:42.150
We're so exhausted.

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:45.510
Like heightened reality of
having shot for so long.

00:46:45.510 --> 00:46:48.990
And then we're doing this
absolutely fantastically absurd

00:46:48.990 --> 00:46:49.698
thing.

00:46:49.698 --> 00:46:51.990
You can see it in the shot
if you're watching us there,

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:54.540
we're all barely holding
on and keeping it together.

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:56.940
It's funny because when I
wrote the original script

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:58.560
I didn't think budget.

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:02.160
And Hedwig was performing in
a lot of different places.

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:05.220
Like a airport lounge.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:06.840
That was going to
be a conveyor belt

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:12.120
camera shot discovering her
in the airport lounge singing.

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:13.830
And then in a rest stop.

00:47:13.830 --> 00:47:15.360
And then "Wig in
a Box" was going

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:20.640
to end up in a garbage scow
barge off of Staten Island

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:21.950
with the helicopter shot.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:26.820
Christine Vachon was
like what if it's

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:31.210
the same restaurant but a
different branch every time?

00:47:31.210 --> 00:47:32.700
I'm like, all right.

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:34.800
And then it was
Bilgewater's, which was fun.

00:47:34.800 --> 00:47:38.340
And we used the same restaurant
and different angles of it.

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:41.460
So it's-- again, the
hermit crab thing.

00:47:41.460 --> 00:47:44.580
You take what you got and
then you'd gussy it up

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:46.680
and that is the spirit of drag.

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:48.150
There was one thing about that.

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:53.280
To me, like, the greatest
moment was the wall

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.040
of the trailer coming down.

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:59.343
When John and Therese and I
were in pre-pro we were talking.

00:47:59.343 --> 00:48:01.260
It was originally written
as a daylight scene.

00:48:01.260 --> 00:48:03.480
And I said, what if it
were a nighttime scene.

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:04.980
Does it matter if
it's day or night?

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:07.740
John's like that would be okay.

00:48:07.740 --> 00:48:09.530
This is such a spectacular--

00:48:09.530 --> 00:48:12.390
when the music changes and
it opens up and goes crazy.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:14.610
And they're shimmying
and shagging, can't we,

00:48:14.610 --> 00:48:18.510
like, blow off the roof and then
have all four walls fall down

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:20.880
and it becomes this
crazy stage or something?

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:22.010
And Therese-- she has to--

00:48:22.010 --> 00:48:23.010
I don't deal with money.

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:24.410
She has to deal with budget.

00:48:24.410 --> 00:48:27.040
And she's like, guys,
we can't do that.

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.000
Okay, fine.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:30.270
But then she said, you know
what, I did a movie once

00:48:30.270 --> 00:48:32.190
where I had one wall come down.

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:33.380
I can give you one wall.

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:35.730
And then John's like, we'll
put footlights on the wall!

00:48:35.730 --> 00:48:38.587
And that just became the
movie and it was brilliant.

00:48:38.587 --> 00:48:40.670
That was, to me, one of
the most charming moments.

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:43.770
Then when we actually came
to the day to film it,

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:46.020
I was very worried about
how am I going to film this

00:48:46.020 --> 00:48:47.580
as a cinematographer.

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:51.660
And I remember, I was having
panic the night before.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:52.830
How am I going to do this?

00:48:52.830 --> 00:48:54.270
This is set up for failure.

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:55.620
Then I put on the song.

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:58.170
I had like a little
tape deck players

00:48:58.170 --> 00:48:59.640
in my hotel room in Toronto.

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:02.370
And I put this song on,
I listen to the song

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:04.860
and I realized no problem.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:05.850
We're going to do this.

00:49:05.850 --> 00:49:06.810
So thank you, Stephen.

00:49:06.810 --> 00:49:08.940
An example of the
rock 'n roll, too--

00:49:08.940 --> 00:49:09.885
Exactly.

00:49:09.885 --> 00:49:13.790
--we're under a power pylon.

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:16.470
The trailer park is under
one of those towers.

00:49:16.470 --> 00:49:21.300
And Frank is again in
the spirit of SqueezeBox,

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:24.480
there's like two
electrical grips.

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:30.390
One with the light and one with
a CD that's reflecting that arc

00:49:30.390 --> 00:49:33.720
lamp onto the pylons.

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:36.390
That's that crazy
light on there.

00:49:36.390 --> 00:49:38.210
It's just a CD.

00:49:38.210 --> 00:49:38.940
It's crazy.

00:49:38.940 --> 00:49:40.950
That was the money
shot, too, I think.

00:49:40.950 --> 00:49:42.060
Totally.

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:46.320
Because that was when you
had the full pull-back.

00:49:46.320 --> 00:49:47.110
The crane?

00:49:47.110 --> 00:49:47.610
Yeah.

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:49.730
We had the hair dress.

00:49:49.730 --> 00:49:52.505
And the animation with
the bouncing ball.

00:49:52.505 --> 00:49:53.080
Totally!

00:49:53.080 --> 00:49:54.204
Good stuff.

00:49:54.204 --> 00:49:55.410
A lot going on.

00:49:55.410 --> 00:50:00.540
Only the crazy, sick, twisted
mind of John and Stephen.

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:03.030
And so Stephen, I want
to ask about shooting

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:04.830
the musical numbers.

00:50:04.830 --> 00:50:07.620
I'm curious, how much,
if any, did the score

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:08.890
change for the film?

00:50:08.890 --> 00:50:11.800
We just rocked harder.

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:14.460
Everything about
it just felt more

00:50:14.460 --> 00:50:16.470
aggressive than on the stage.

00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:17.980
It was more guitar heavy.

00:50:17.980 --> 00:50:18.753
Little more punk.

00:50:18.753 --> 00:50:20.670
And what was one of the
things that was really

00:50:20.670 --> 00:50:23.080
fun for me besides
rerecording the songs

00:50:23.080 --> 00:50:26.100
and getting a different
sound was the actors

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:30.570
all learned to play the
instruments of the people

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:32.140
that they were playing.

00:50:32.140 --> 00:50:34.320
Obviously, I was
playing my own parts.

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:35.520
Bob Mould was not in it.

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:39.330
Ted Liscinski, who played
bass on the recording,

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:41.820
played the guitar player,
and so Bob taught him

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:42.945
how to play all the parts.

00:50:45.622 --> 00:50:47.080
The guy who played
the bass player,

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:48.840
Ted taught him how to
play all the stuff.

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:53.040
They came to the rehearsals,
and Michael learned

00:50:53.040 --> 00:50:54.270
how to play the drum parts.

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:56.880
He knew every drum part.

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.105
The way that we did it with all
the musicians actually looking

00:51:01.105 --> 00:51:02.730
like they were playing
the instruments.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:04.980
And then the monitors that
they had next to them that

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:06.605
looked like they were
listening to them

00:51:06.605 --> 00:51:11.280
were actually playing their
part out into the house.

00:51:11.280 --> 00:51:14.340
And so then we had this audience
of extras who actually believed

00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:15.700
they were at a concert.

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:17.910
So we were there
doing it and they

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:21.990
were responding like they
were seeing a band perform.

00:51:21.990 --> 00:51:23.670
And none of us were
actually playing.

00:51:23.670 --> 00:51:27.930
But it gave that impression.

00:51:27.930 --> 00:51:32.010
It felt very magical
every time we did it.

00:51:32.010 --> 00:51:33.450
Just really quickly,
can everybody

00:51:33.450 --> 00:51:35.880
tell me your favorite
moments or memories

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:38.080
from the shoot and the film?

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:41.550
I would say Therese and I
were next to the camera,

00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:44.100
and John was singing
'Origin of Love'

00:51:44.100 --> 00:51:47.460
and we both were crying by
the time the song ended,

00:51:47.460 --> 00:51:51.340
holding each other,
and it was very sweet.

00:51:51.340 --> 00:51:52.510
Yeah.

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:54.970
Mine was every
day at lunch, John

00:51:54.970 --> 00:51:58.660
would have to shave
his face in full drag.

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:00.767
And we would never know
was the make-up going

00:52:00.767 --> 00:52:02.480
to-- what was going to happen.

00:52:02.480 --> 00:52:04.360
We would shoot all the
close-ups at the end,

00:52:04.360 --> 00:52:06.800
and it was always,
like, 40 filters.

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:09.760
But I just remember
John every day at lunch.

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:10.750
Full drag.

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:13.435
And directing in the chair,
but he would just shave.

00:52:13.435 --> 00:52:15.560
You can shave through
make-up if it's an electric--

00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:16.210
Yes.

00:52:16.210 --> 00:52:16.930
You can.

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:18.640
I have to say that last shoot.

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:24.370
That last 'Wig in a Box' moment
with us singing the song,

00:52:24.370 --> 00:52:26.350
and it was the end of the shoot.

00:52:26.350 --> 00:52:28.180
And we sort of knew
that, and was this sort

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:30.820
of really magical moment.

00:52:30.820 --> 00:52:33.010
And we're all just insane
by the end of the night

00:52:33.010 --> 00:52:34.990
and the end of the shoot.

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:39.070
That, and then also the moment
where Hedwig gives Yitzhak

00:52:39.070 --> 00:52:41.890
the wig that we shot.

00:52:41.890 --> 00:52:45.370
That was just a really-- it's
always an emotional moment

00:52:45.370 --> 00:52:46.120
playing Yitzhak.

00:52:46.120 --> 00:52:50.110
But it was so beautiful
the way that it was shot.

00:52:50.110 --> 00:52:52.230
The whiteness-- Therese--

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:53.980
I mean just the look
of it in that moment.

00:52:53.980 --> 00:52:55.780
And then people catching
me as I fell back,

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:57.760
and then I got to be in drag.

00:52:57.760 --> 00:53:00.400
I felt like an alien angel.

00:53:00.400 --> 00:53:01.930
It was just this combination.

00:53:01.930 --> 00:53:04.115
So it was a really
meta moment where

00:53:04.115 --> 00:53:05.740
I felt like Yitzhak
was getting a dream

00:53:05.740 --> 00:53:08.080
and I was also getting a
dream to sort of come true

00:53:08.080 --> 00:53:09.200
in that moment.

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:09.950
I just remembered.

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:11.325
There is actually
one song, which

00:53:11.325 --> 00:53:14.750
is 'Hedwig's Lament'
which was the one time,

00:53:14.750 --> 00:53:16.790
it felt like everything
had stripped down

00:53:16.790 --> 00:53:21.640
and John and I are
both performing live.

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:25.060
The piano that you hear is
me playing whatever piano

00:53:25.060 --> 00:53:26.500
it was that happened--

00:53:26.500 --> 00:53:28.420
I don't even know if
we tested that piano.

00:53:28.420 --> 00:53:30.670
It was not, like, the most
awesome piano in the world,

00:53:30.670 --> 00:53:33.550
but it was perfect
for that song.

00:53:33.550 --> 00:53:37.320
In that whole like
production where everything

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:41.280
just kind of blew up into
much larger proportions

00:53:41.280 --> 00:53:43.440
and then just before
it blew up there

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:45.480
was this one moment
where it like

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:49.090
zeroed in on just John and
me performing live together,

00:53:49.090 --> 00:53:53.750
which somehow feels
really special to me.

00:53:53.750 --> 00:53:56.720
I had a lot more fun directing
other people than myself.

00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:59.000
I think in the moment during
Tommy's 'Wicked Little

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:03.660
Town' when Tommy is singing
to Hedwig in this black void.

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:08.090
And I was able to let
go, you know, and cry.

00:54:08.090 --> 00:54:10.250
There had been so much tension.

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:12.455
I don't think I'd taken
a shit for a month.

00:54:12.455 --> 00:54:14.547
I had an anal fissure.

00:54:14.547 --> 00:54:15.830
Ouch.

00:54:15.830 --> 00:54:16.330
Okay.

00:54:16.330 --> 00:54:17.790
I shouldn't have
started laughing.

00:54:17.790 --> 00:54:18.320
Hello.

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:21.170
Ouch.

00:54:21.170 --> 00:54:24.560
It was such a journey,
harder than anything

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:29.080
I will ever do before or
since or in the future.

00:54:29.080 --> 00:54:30.830
Even though I didn't
really want to act in

00:54:30.830 --> 00:54:32.780
that because I had
done that, that

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:37.160
was an opportunity
to cry in a good way.

00:54:37.160 --> 00:54:40.220
Hedwig is crying
for the boyfriend

00:54:40.220 --> 00:54:43.550
finally apologizing
and letting go.

00:54:43.550 --> 00:54:45.440
Letting her go.

00:54:45.440 --> 00:54:47.913
Him being let go, as well.

00:54:47.913 --> 00:54:49.580
And that's the beginning
of forgiveness,

00:54:49.580 --> 00:54:51.480
because Hedwig is a victim.

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:53.270
Acts like a victim throughout.

00:54:53.270 --> 00:54:54.710
I'm a fucking victim.

00:54:54.710 --> 00:54:57.020
And is very mean to
people because of that.

00:54:57.020 --> 00:54:59.490
I got hurt, therefore
I'm going to hurt you.

00:54:59.490 --> 00:55:02.950
I'm justified in
hurting, which is what

00:55:02.950 --> 00:55:04.310
so many people feel right now.

00:55:04.310 --> 00:55:06.800
It's like everyone is
a professional victim.

00:55:06.800 --> 00:55:09.500
And I admire the
people who create

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:11.360
the professional alternatives.

00:55:11.360 --> 00:55:13.310
Yes, we know there
are grievances,

00:55:13.310 --> 00:55:14.850
we know there's injustice.

00:55:14.850 --> 00:55:16.010
What are you going to do?

00:55:16.010 --> 00:55:20.730
Rather than saying you're
hurting me or I hate you,

00:55:20.730 --> 00:55:22.200
you're canceled.

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:26.270
It's like how do we create
an environment where when we

00:55:26.270 --> 00:55:32.870
find the things in common, where
we allow people to even change?

00:55:32.870 --> 00:55:36.500
But I really think
Hedwig's story

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:40.040
has resonated for more people
than we expected, because we've

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:41.580
all felt put upon.

00:55:41.580 --> 00:55:43.040
We've all felt like a victim.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:46.100
And if you stay in that
mode, even if you're

00:55:46.100 --> 00:55:48.590
the most powerful
victim, you're still

00:55:48.590 --> 00:55:50.570
under the power of the
person who hurt you.

00:55:50.570 --> 00:55:53.780
And you've got to
find a way to forgive.

00:55:53.780 --> 00:55:57.260
And if they don't take the
forgiveness, then you move on.

00:55:57.260 --> 00:56:03.135
John, you said that Hedwig
is not a trans story.

00:56:03.135 --> 00:56:04.760
And you talked a
little bit about this.

00:56:04.760 --> 00:56:07.910
But can you go in a little
bit on what Hedwig is about

00:56:07.910 --> 00:56:09.650
if it's not a trans
story for people

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:12.170
who might describe it as such?

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:13.670
Yeah, I really don't see it as--

00:56:13.670 --> 00:56:16.950
I don't think that's the
best way to look at it.

00:56:16.950 --> 00:56:23.430
It is a story of this boy
Hansel who is in a lot of cages.

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:25.710
There's the cage
of East Germany.

00:56:25.710 --> 00:56:29.870
There's a place that is
not welcoming to his very

00:56:29.870 --> 00:56:31.730
creative queer side.

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:35.990
He is in a cage of
masculinity that he feels

00:56:35.990 --> 00:56:39.590
there's more space for more--

00:56:39.590 --> 00:56:42.140
femininity, whatever
you want to call it.

00:56:42.140 --> 00:56:46.910
It's a person who is raped
as a child, which is implied,

00:56:46.910 --> 00:56:48.260
raped by his father.

00:56:48.260 --> 00:56:51.170
There is abuse throughout.

00:56:51.170 --> 00:56:53.390
Ultimately, there
is this operation

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:56.880
that he's kind of forced into
by his boyfriend and mother.

00:56:56.880 --> 00:56:58.610
And, in fact, his government.

00:56:58.610 --> 00:57:02.700
To be free, you have
to change your gender.

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:05.510
And this is how we
define your gender.

00:57:05.510 --> 00:57:07.340
No penis means you're a woman.

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:10.760
What a horrible way
to define femininity

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:12.770
is-- with a negative.

00:57:12.770 --> 00:57:16.310
And the inch being
a metaphor for what

00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:20.840
is left of that abuse,
what is the scar,

00:57:20.840 --> 00:57:24.200
the remnant of these
people having hurt him.

00:57:24.200 --> 00:57:28.490
And when he does end up
in a trailer in Kansas,

00:57:28.490 --> 00:57:32.990
alone, abandoned by that man
who in effect mutilated him

00:57:32.990 --> 00:57:36.830
without much consensual
choice, not allowing him,

00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:39.560
as Stephen said, to go on
his own gender journey,

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:43.550
but forcing it, that's
an example of top

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:49.310
down, forcing people
into these narrow places

00:57:49.310 --> 00:57:50.900
they didn't expect to be.

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:53.000
We all end up in
these narrow places,

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:55.250
and we've all had
some bruises and,

00:57:55.250 --> 00:57:58.610
hopefully, not as
horrible wounds

00:57:58.610 --> 00:58:00.470
that Hedwig has experienced.

00:58:00.470 --> 00:58:03.410
And then there is the next
step, which, Peppermint,

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:07.190
you identified as self-creation
to me, which is like,

00:58:07.190 --> 00:58:11.900
in effect, in drag, which is
a tool that anyone can use.

00:58:11.900 --> 00:58:19.130
Straight, cis, queer, trans
can use drag in a special way

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:24.290
to defend yourself with armor,
but also to create a canvas

00:58:24.290 --> 00:58:28.020
to express yourself safely.

00:58:28.020 --> 00:58:31.740
That is when Hedwig puts on
the make-up, finds that wig,

00:58:31.740 --> 00:58:34.680
and makes something
beautiful and new.

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:36.360
Which is more than
a man and a woman.

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:39.480
It is a gender of one.

00:58:39.480 --> 00:58:41.070
And so that's why
I don't think--

00:58:41.070 --> 00:58:47.280
I mean, it's a trans in
terms of crossing boundaries.

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:48.090
Yes.

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:51.910
But not necessarily the
traditional definition

00:58:51.910 --> 00:58:56.140
of transgender, which is I'm
finding myself through agency,

00:58:56.140 --> 00:59:00.520
through volition, through
sometimes physical changes,

00:59:00.520 --> 00:59:02.860
sometimes just identification.

00:59:02.860 --> 00:59:07.210
But the deeper elements
of finding yourself

00:59:07.210 --> 00:59:10.300
through the woods,
through the abuse,

00:59:10.300 --> 00:59:14.740
is one that trans
friends have told me

00:59:14.740 --> 00:59:17.620
they've found use in Hedwig.

00:59:17.620 --> 00:59:23.280
Cis friends, queer friends,
gay friends, straight folks.

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:25.770
Outsiders, all.

00:59:25.770 --> 00:59:29.340
And to limit the
character being played

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:35.880
by one category or another goes
against its actual message,

00:59:35.880 --> 00:59:41.130
which is self-realization and
finding your own wholeness.

00:59:41.130 --> 00:59:44.700
In the end, Hedwig doesn't even
need the tool of drag anymore.

00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:48.330
And in horror and
frustration, tears it off

00:59:48.330 --> 00:59:56.550
and walks into the world naked
with a scar saying take me

00:59:56.550 --> 01:00:00.240
as I am or don't take me at all.

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:02.400
I no longer need this defense.

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:07.920
And I see Hedwig now as
probably a non-binary professor

01:00:07.920 --> 01:00:10.680
of German philosophy
and rock 'n roll

01:00:10.680 --> 01:00:15.540
in a small Midwestern
college about to lose her job

01:00:15.540 --> 01:00:16.890
and healthcare.

01:00:16.890 --> 01:00:19.050
I will take that
course any time.

01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:23.380
I want to ask each of you about
the enduring legacy of Hedwig

01:00:23.380 --> 01:00:28.620
and when you became
aware that Hedwig

01:00:28.620 --> 01:00:30.638
had become such a cult classic.

01:00:30.638 --> 01:00:32.430
I think the first time
I knew it was a cult

01:00:32.430 --> 01:00:34.830
hit was when Michael,
who is my husband,

01:00:34.830 --> 01:00:37.770
got a job at the University
of Kentucky in 2004.

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:39.390
I started going to clubs.

01:00:39.390 --> 01:00:43.110
I met this guy, Duane
Lundy, who owned

01:00:43.110 --> 01:00:45.240
a recording studio in town.

01:00:45.240 --> 01:00:48.330
And he was working with a
band called Parlour Boys,

01:00:48.330 --> 01:00:50.460
and I met him at
one of their shows

01:00:50.460 --> 01:00:51.660
and he was recording them.

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:53.413
And he was already
a huge Hedwig fan.

01:00:53.413 --> 01:00:55.080
Then I started hanging
out with the band

01:00:55.080 --> 01:00:59.930
and I became friends with them,
and everyone in their circle

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:06.860
of friends who are like very
creative and very progressive

01:01:06.860 --> 01:01:09.980
millennials, and they
all knew the movie.

01:01:09.980 --> 01:01:12.260
They had all watched
it in high school.

01:01:12.260 --> 01:01:13.550
Everybody knew it.

01:01:13.550 --> 01:01:16.050
They were all big fans.

01:01:16.050 --> 01:01:18.710
And then I started going
to clubs to hear bands

01:01:18.710 --> 01:01:20.900
and I'd meet people
at the clubs.

01:01:20.900 --> 01:01:24.350
And they're always like 24,
and they'd be like Oh my God --

01:01:24.350 --> 01:01:26.918
I'm such a huge fan.

01:01:26.918 --> 01:01:28.460
We were in Lexington,
Kentucky, and I

01:01:28.460 --> 01:01:31.910
was like, I didn't have
any clue that was going

01:01:31.910 --> 01:01:34.640
on in the country as a whole.

01:01:34.640 --> 01:01:38.810
For me the legacy
personally is that Hedwig

01:01:38.810 --> 01:01:42.410
changed the trajectory
of my choices

01:01:42.410 --> 01:01:49.220
that I've made in my career
and in my artistic life.

01:01:49.220 --> 01:01:56.550
And it re-calibrated who I
went into blindly pursuing.

01:01:56.550 --> 01:01:59.400
And so I'm forever
grateful for that.

01:01:59.400 --> 01:02:04.340
And on the larger level, it
has fostered a conversation

01:02:04.340 --> 01:02:10.820
that I have with strangers about
meaning and purpose and joy

01:02:10.820 --> 01:02:15.290
and celebration and pride
in so many different forms

01:02:15.290 --> 01:02:19.120
and a connection to strangers.

01:02:19.120 --> 01:02:21.710
And then the physical
legacy is that it

01:02:21.710 --> 01:02:24.800
was the only film I ever
owned the costumes for, which

01:02:24.800 --> 01:02:27.560
was the deal I made with
Christine Vachon and Katie

01:02:27.560 --> 01:02:29.690
Roumel, the producers,
because they couldn't pay me

01:02:29.690 --> 01:02:30.400
enough money.

01:02:30.400 --> 01:02:32.640
I said, oaky, I'm
keeping the costumes.

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:35.870
And I have donated
almost all the costumes

01:02:35.870 --> 01:02:38.210
to the AMPAS museum.

01:02:38.210 --> 01:02:39.920
So they have a permanent home.

01:02:39.920 --> 01:02:42.800
I've donated all my sketches
and will live on and hopefully

01:02:42.800 --> 01:02:45.080
that physical
legacy, people will

01:02:45.080 --> 01:02:49.890
get to enjoy the costumes
in future exhibitions.

01:02:49.890 --> 01:02:52.080
And so I'm super
grateful for that.

01:02:52.080 --> 01:02:56.750
I keep meeting people
every day who were affected

01:02:56.750 --> 01:03:00.980
by Hedwig here in Brisbane.

01:03:00.980 --> 01:03:04.160
I was trying to get
my rent stabilized

01:03:04.160 --> 01:03:06.890
apartment form notarised
here in Brisbane.

01:03:06.890 --> 01:03:09.180
And the secretary
is like, you know

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:14.090
you have the same name
as an actor on Broadway.

01:03:14.090 --> 01:03:15.950
Have you heard of Hedwig
and the Angry Inch?

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:16.950
And I'm like, that's me.

01:03:16.950 --> 01:03:18.710
And she's like, no way!

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:24.050
So it filters not
in a massive kind

01:03:24.050 --> 01:03:30.020
of shoved down your throat by
marketing Marvel Universe way.

01:03:30.020 --> 01:03:32.600
It bubbles up from the bottom.

01:03:32.600 --> 01:03:35.330
It's passed on word of mouth.

01:03:35.330 --> 01:03:38.270
A person gives it to
a friend, the DVD,

01:03:38.270 --> 01:03:41.420
or now it's just streaming.

01:03:41.420 --> 01:03:43.850
I've heard of multiple
people saying,

01:03:43.850 --> 01:03:47.280
when I start dating someone I
have shown the film to them.

01:03:47.280 --> 01:03:50.090
And if they don't like it,
we don't continue dating.

01:03:50.090 --> 01:03:53.630
You know, I love being
a dating litmus test.

01:03:53.630 --> 01:03:57.020
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:57.020 --> 01:04:02.340
And it kind of resonates, again,
throughout sexualities, ages,

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:04.880
genders, race.

01:04:04.880 --> 01:04:08.990
There's a sense of understanding
when you've been knocked down

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:11.790
and you're trying to put
yourself back together.

01:04:11.790 --> 01:04:12.890
Find your wholeness.

01:04:12.890 --> 01:04:16.910
Find that other half, even if
it's an internal other half.

01:04:16.910 --> 01:04:20.510
And it's found its way--

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:26.400
the countries that have welcomed
it most, Korea, Japan, Spain,

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:29.010
Australia, France.

01:04:29.010 --> 01:04:32.120
These are places we
didn't expect to resonate

01:04:32.120 --> 01:04:33.770
with something unusual.

01:04:33.770 --> 01:04:40.310
Because Korea and Japan aren't
super queer-relaxed places.

01:04:40.310 --> 01:04:44.590
But the story itself,
especially Korea--

01:04:44.590 --> 01:04:47.717
I'm actually a
rock star in Korea.

01:04:47.717 --> 01:04:50.300
Stephen and I can go there and
perform to 10,000 people in one

01:04:50.300 --> 01:04:50.990
night.

01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:56.060
And K-pop stars show up and
they're like, we love Hedwig!

01:04:56.060 --> 01:04:59.580
Partly, their flag is
the yin yang symbol,

01:04:59.580 --> 01:05:01.220
which is similar to
our Hedwig symbol.

01:05:01.220 --> 01:05:05.300
That it is a broken nation,
just like Germany was broken.

01:05:05.300 --> 01:05:08.930
So the queer stuff aside, we
get so many people resonating

01:05:08.930 --> 01:05:13.640
to different parts of the story,
which is so gratifying to us.

01:05:13.640 --> 01:05:16.460
And the people I've met
just because of this project

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:17.810
are some of my favorite people.

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:19.070
It keeps giving.

01:05:19.070 --> 01:05:22.760
When I meet a
trans kid in Sydney

01:05:22.760 --> 01:05:24.950
who's just coming
up and saying you

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:28.160
gave me a little bit of hope
that there was some space

01:05:28.160 --> 01:05:29.990
for me to make myself.

01:05:29.990 --> 01:05:32.990
Not make myself in the
image of Hedwig or you,

01:05:32.990 --> 01:05:35.510
but make my own journey.

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:38.660
That is the most
empowering compliment

01:05:38.660 --> 01:05:40.745
any artist can have
is that it made

01:05:40.745 --> 01:05:43.370
someone feel that they could do
something they didn't know they

01:05:43.370 --> 01:05:45.590
could that has helped them.

01:05:45.590 --> 01:05:49.020
I think that it's a piece
of art about identity,

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:51.110
about national identity, right?

01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:53.330
It's one of the only
pieces of American art

01:05:53.330 --> 01:05:56.180
I know of that deals with
the Berlin Wall falling.

01:05:56.180 --> 01:05:58.430
And the Berlin Wall
was this moment

01:05:58.430 --> 01:05:59.700
of identity for everyone.

01:05:59.700 --> 01:06:01.400
People all over the
world identified

01:06:01.400 --> 01:06:03.800
who they were as nations based
on which side of the wall

01:06:03.800 --> 01:06:04.970
they were on.

01:06:04.970 --> 01:06:07.430
And we sort of forget that
now because it's gone.

01:06:07.430 --> 01:06:09.110
It just dissipated
and disappeared,

01:06:09.110 --> 01:06:12.740
but it was how people define
themselves as nations.

01:06:12.740 --> 01:06:13.250
Binary.

01:06:13.250 --> 01:06:13.750
Yeah.

01:06:13.750 --> 01:06:16.610
And that same split.

01:06:16.610 --> 01:06:19.790
So unnatural to split
something and say pick a side.

01:06:19.790 --> 01:06:22.500
You cannot go-- there's a
no man's land in between.

01:06:22.500 --> 01:06:25.700
You don't get to go
over it or you'll die.

01:06:25.700 --> 01:06:30.200
That kind of feeling of
searching for identity,

01:06:30.200 --> 01:06:32.990
it was so beautifully
put into this piece that

01:06:32.990 --> 01:06:38.810
was like East, West Germany
but also male, female yin, yang

01:06:38.810 --> 01:06:41.120
and who are you in that world?

01:06:41.120 --> 01:06:42.500
Where do you exist?

01:06:42.500 --> 01:06:44.400
When do you get to
make that decision?

01:06:44.400 --> 01:06:46.790
And I feel like Hedwig
asked that question

01:06:46.790 --> 01:06:52.190
with music, which is the most
visceral way to ask a question.

01:06:52.190 --> 01:06:56.900
And people just responded to it
because it asked that question

01:06:56.900 --> 01:06:58.460
of you when you watch it.

01:06:58.460 --> 01:07:01.040
And some people maybe it
felt like the first time

01:07:01.040 --> 01:07:02.450
they were allowed to answer.

01:07:02.450 --> 01:07:07.520
In the last 20 years, John,
since the movie was released,

01:07:07.520 --> 01:07:09.926
what would Hedwig think?

01:07:09.926 --> 01:07:11.360
Hedwig would be
where's my money?

01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:13.850
[LAUGHTER]

01:07:13.850 --> 01:07:15.650
I'm playing Joe
Exotic right now,

01:07:15.650 --> 01:07:18.260
and there's a lot in
common with Hedwig.

01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:18.920
Right?

01:07:18.920 --> 01:07:25.490
Sudden fame, jealousy,
anger, young boyfriends.

01:07:25.490 --> 01:07:28.490
You know, before the pandemic,
I would have not understood

01:07:28.490 --> 01:07:32.150
that, but now I totally get it.

01:07:32.150 --> 01:07:33.770
What do you think, Stephen?

01:07:33.770 --> 01:07:35.110
What would Hedwig be doing now?

01:07:37.430 --> 01:07:40.070
I think she's saying that
there's too many walls, not

01:07:40.070 --> 01:07:41.570
enough bridges.

01:07:41.570 --> 01:07:42.850
That's right.

01:07:42.850 --> 01:07:45.050
You find a point of
connection between you

01:07:45.050 --> 01:07:48.570
and another person,
or another group.

01:07:48.570 --> 01:07:51.590
And you can make it into
a wall or you can make it

01:07:51.590 --> 01:07:53.530
into a bridge, and
Hedwig would prefer

01:07:53.530 --> 01:07:55.050
to make it into a bridge.

01:07:55.050 --> 01:07:55.550
OK.

01:07:55.550 --> 01:08:01.910
So I want to say thank you to
each of you for spending time.

01:08:01.910 --> 01:08:04.138
I want to say thank you
to everyone watching

01:08:04.138 --> 01:08:06.680
and, of course, I want to say
thank you to the Academy, which

01:08:06.680 --> 01:08:08.030
I've always wanted to say.

01:08:08.030 --> 01:08:10.790
I have no business, but
I'm going to say it!

01:08:10.790 --> 01:08:16.340
Now, John, Stephen, Arianne,
Mike, Miriam, Frankie,

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:18.060
thank you so much.

01:08:18.060 --> 01:08:20.600
Congratulations, to each of you.

01:08:20.600 --> 01:08:25.279
And to everyone that had
anything to do with this film,

01:08:25.279 --> 01:08:30.319
with the Off-Broadway, with the
workshop, with the West End,

01:08:30.319 --> 01:08:32.689
with the Broadway
production, any iteration.

01:08:32.689 --> 01:08:34.790
And, of course, the
many, many people

01:08:34.790 --> 01:08:38.930
who have the fortune to work
on and be associated with any

01:08:38.930 --> 01:08:41.359
of the subsequent
productions that

01:08:41.359 --> 01:08:43.819
have been on stage, thereafter.

01:08:43.819 --> 01:08:47.930
Congratulations to 20
years and thank you so much

01:08:47.930 --> 01:08:52.189
on behalf of so many people who
are gender expansive, gender

01:08:52.189 --> 01:08:55.710
explorative, and beyond.

01:08:55.710 --> 01:08:56.660
Thank you, Peppermint.

01:08:56.660 --> 01:08:57.620
We love you!

01:08:57.620 --> 01:08:58.880
Peppermint, we love you!

01:08:58.880 --> 01:09:00.090
Thank you.

01:09:00.090 --> 01:09:01.180
I love you.

01:09:01.180 --> 01:09:03.020
Love you.

01:09:03.020 --> 01:09:05.029
Thank you.

01:09:05.029 --> 01:09:09.879
[MUSIC PLAYING]

